# FIRST-POWER-ON OVERLAY. NOT A BUILD VARIANT. NEVER SHIPS.
#
# Layered on top of sdkconfig.defaults + sdkconfig.dev for one purpose: proving
# a brand-new Walter boots, enumerates, and answers `hello` before anything
# irreversible happens to it.
#
# What it turns off and why:
#
# sdkconfig.defaults sets CONFIG_SECURE_FLASH_ENC_ENABLED=y in DEVELOPMENT mode
# (D-4 — this device will get lost, so encryption at rest is the baseline). The
# cost is that the FIRST boot of such an image generates a key, burns it plus
# SPI_BOOT_CRYPT_CNT into eFuse, and encrypts flash in place. eFuse burns do not
# come back. Doing that to the only board on the bench, during the session whose
# entire question is "does this board work at all", means every later failure
# has two candidate causes instead of one.
#
# The implementation plan already says where that belongs: wave 5B validates
# "secure boot v2 + flash encryption on a sacrificial board first"
# (chaski-implementation-plan §5B). This overlay is what keeps bring-up on the
# near side of that line.
#
# NVS encryption goes with it because it is not independently selectable — it
# requires flash encryption, and leaving it on here would fail the build rather
# than do anything useful. The `encrypted` flag on the letters partition is
# inert with flash encryption off; the partition table needs no change.
#
# Consequences while this overlay is in use, so nothing is mistaken for a pass:
#   - D-4 is NOT satisfied. Flash and NVS are plaintext, readable off the chip.
#   - Any letter stored during a run with this overlay is at rest in the clear.
#     Bench letters only. Never a real one.
#   - C-23 (the fuse ceremony) and the wave 5B encryption checks say nothing
#     about a board that never burned a fuse.
#
# Drop this overlay — build with just "sdkconfig.defaults;sdkconfig.dev" — as
# soon as the board is known good and you are ready for the fuses to burn.

CONFIG_SECURE_FLASH_ENC_ENABLED=n
CONFIG_NVS_ENCRYPTION=n
